Criteria recorded before reading native.md.
Sources: normative spec §§2.4,4.1,4.3,4.7–4.8,6.7,9.2,9.4–9.7,10.3,11,B; accepted syntax; touched rules in records/value-types/types-joint/meta/std-1/host-concurrency/lazy-reentry/runtime/multicore; plan principles and user constraints.
1. Resource abstraction: a client cannot manufacture a private nominal resource from Int, or extract/replace a raw service function to cause the module to wrap a fabricated Int. Try direct constructor, type alias, service .field, fake environment, service reconstruction/update, external/host aliases. Trusted host authority is separate from ordinary source authority.
2. Classify every restriction: existing spec rule, general new rule with three unrelated uses and principal inference, or forbidden library exception. Name-only exports imply no deconstruction; structural records must not become nominal views. Hidden service environments must remain usable and ordinary library tests possible.
3. Multicore preserves sequential step semantics, identity, lexical captures, dynamic environments, local witnesses, task paths/random, scope membership, cancellation/finals and FIFO M:1. Claim/cancel/publication is exactly once; no callback under shared lock; prepared bytes revalidate liveness.
4. Teardown: stop during a spin or sync import; request completed but undelivered; malformed reply during decode; Ready but untaken resource; cancellation during close; close while other worker reads/waits; late spawn after teardown; failed termination/reaping; reused host/factory with two simultaneous or sequential runs; no cross-run numeric alias or interruption.
5. Requests/Owner/Root remain typed, generation safe, worker local for Request/Ticket but usable with shared portable groups. Progress/deadlock includes cross-worker request counts without foreign GC references.
6. General host grants must bind declaration identity and both external/service routes, survive metadata and compiler-generated origins, and have a credible trusted-source boundary without altering source nominal identity or requiring resource-specific compiler hooks.
7. R1/R2/IO can use a stable contract now. Distinguish implementation not yet built from missing design; inspect cited paths/commits. Every new prerequisite must be in phase gates. Compare runtime/multicore/native rules and replacement text, not just adoption ledger.
